{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "11",
    "case_summary": "A 41-year-old female with a history of NF1 presented with a 3-year history of recurrent epigastric soreness, heartburn, and diarrhea. Endoscopic examinations repeatedly showed recurrent duodenal ulcers. Symptoms were alleviated with proton-pump inhibitors but returned upon cessation of the medication. The patient had a family history of NF1, with her brother, sister, and second son also exhibiting fully developed features of the condition. She exhibited normal intellectual development without cognitive dysfunction or learning disabilities. Physical examination identified several café-au-lait spots, multiple small nodules on the anterior chest and areolar area, and multiple axillary freckles. A biopsy of a skin nodule confirmed neurofibromas. During admission, upper gastrointestinal endoscopy revealed multiple shallow ulcers in the descending duodenum. Tests for Helicobacter pylori, including the rapid urease test and urea breath test, were negative.\n\nThe serum fasting gastrin level was >1,000 pg/mL and 837 pg/mL in two consecutive measurements. Abdominal computed tomography (CT) and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scans showed a 3 × 2-cm, clearly defined, well-enhanced mass adjacent to the duodenal loop in the subhepatic space. No metastatic lesions were observed in the liver or regional lymph nodes.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
